      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The major hidden cost of this early global connectivity was the rapid transmission of disease, most notably the devastating spread of the bubonic plague along the established trade routes. Beyond biological threats, reliance on these long-distance supply lines created vulnerabilities; political instability or warfare in one region could immediately disrupt vital trade and resource flows elsewhere. This interconnectedness essentially meant that prosperity and peril could travel at the same speed.",
      "source": "target",
